\chapter{Agreement between screeners}
\label{agreement-module}

A review that screens its records twice keeps the second screener's
verdicts beside the decisions, as \emph{screenings}
(\cref{sec:screenings} in the review module): a classifier's call
checked by an agent, say, or an agent's checked by a second human.  The
record alone answers neither of the two questions a double screen is
for (\url{https://github.com/dbosk/scholarcli/issues/253}): how often
did the screeners agree, and where did they not?  This chapter answers
both from the stored screenings, without changing anything: the
decision stays the outcome, and nothing here writes to a session.

The chapter has five parts, each used by the next.
\begin{description}
\item[Screeners] (\cref{sec:agreement-screeners}) who a screening is
  from, and which of a screener's verdicts of a paper counts.
\item[The agreement table] (\cref{sec:agreement-table}) the counts of a
  pair of screeners' verdicts, and the measures of agreement computed
  from them, with the sources that define them.
\item[Three questions] (\cref{sec:agreement-questions}) kept or
  discarded, bearing on the claim or not, and the category; the pairs
  of screeners a set of records holds.
\item[Samples] (\cref{sec:agreement-samples}) the agreement over a
  stored random sample, the one case with an interval.
\item[Conflicts] (\cref{sec:agreement-conflicts}) the papers where two
  screeners disagree, and whether a human has settled them.
\end{description}
The chapter ends with the text the commands print
(\cref{sec:agreement-text}).  Three commands read it: [[sessions
agreement]] prints the tables, [[sessions conflicts]] lists the
conflicts, and [[sessions show]] adds a few summary lines (all in the
CLI module); the TUI has a filter for the unresolved conflicts.

