\section{Screeners and their verdicts}
\label{sec:agreement-screeners}

A screener is who made a screening, as the review module tells
screeners apart ([[screener_identity]], \cref{sec:screenings}): its
kind ([[llm]], [[agent]] or [[human]]), its name as stored in the
record, and whether the record is a classifier's second screen that
Scholar ran.  The last part keeps a model's second screen apart from
the snapshot of a decision the same model made, which has the same
kind and name; the review module says why a same-model second screen
is recorded rather than refused.  Two records with the same identity
are the same screener, wherever in the session they are; a name is
matched as written, so two sessions pooled into one report share a
screener only when they spell it alike.  The prompt version of a
classifier is not part of it: a model asked with a revised prompt is
the same screener, and the text lists the prompt versions that occur
instead.  This module uses that one function wherever it compares
screeners, so that a pair, a group and a label never disagree on who
is who.

A screener without a name is a screener of its own: an agent that
gave no name, or a snapshot of a decision made before names were kept
(\cref{sec:screenings} in the review module: [[sessions screen]]
requires a name, but a snapshot copies whatever the decision holds).
All unnamed screeners of one kind are one screener, since nothing tells
them apart, and the text calls it \enquote{unnamed agent}.  A snapshot
whose kind is not known, of a decision with a source Scholar does not
know, is no screener at all; it is counted, as a verdict of unknown
origin, but never paired.

<<agreement screeners>>=
Screener = tuple  # screener_identity: (kind, name or None, second screen)


def screener_of(record: dict) -> Screener | None:
    """The screener of a screening record, or None if its kind is unknown."""
    if record.get("kind") not in SCREENING_KINDS:
        return None
    return screener_identity(record)
@

The commands name a screener on the command line ([[--pair]]) as
[[kind:name]], or as the kind alone for the unnamed one, and the text
prints that form beside each pair so that it can be copied.  A
classifier's second screen adds [[+second]] to the kind
([[llm+second:model-x]]): the kind is one of a few fixed words, so the
mark cannot be confused with a name, which may hold anything, a colon
included; only the first colon separates.

<<agreement screeners>>=
SECOND_SCREEN_MARK = "+second"


def screener_spec(screener: Screener) -> str:
    """How `--pair` names `screener`: `kind[+second]:name`, or no name."""
    kind, name, second = screener
    kind += SECOND_SCREEN_MARK if second else ""
    return f"{kind}:{name}" if name is not None else kind


def parse_screener_spec(text: str) -> Screener:
    """The screener that `kind[+second]:name` (or no `:name`) names.

    Raises:
        ValueError: The kind is not one of `SCREENING_KINDS`, or the name
            after the colon is empty.
    """
    kind, colon, name = text.strip().partition(":")
    second = kind.endswith(SECOND_SCREEN_MARK)
    if second:
        kind = kind[: -len(SECOND_SCREEN_MARK)]
    if kind not in SCREENING_KINDS:
        raise ValueError(
            f"{text!r} names no screener kind (give one of "
            f"{', '.join(SCREENING_KINDS)}, as kind:name)"
        )
    if colon and not name:
        raise ValueError(f"{text!r} has an empty name after the colon")
    return (kind, name if colon else None, second)
@

[[--pair]] takes two screeners separated by a comma, and a name may
hold a comma (\enquote{Doe, Jane}).  The separating comma is the one
followed by a kind, with or without the second-screen mark, and then a
colon or the end: a kind is one of a few fixed words, so such a comma
is rarely inside a name.  When more than one comma qualifies, or none,
the pair is refused with a message rather than split by guess.  A
screener compared with itself would agree on every record, so a pair
of one screener twice is refused too.  The header of a pair prints the
option as it would be typed, quoted for the shell
(\cref{sec:agreement-text}), so a name with a comma or a space can be
copied.

<<agreement screeners>>=
_PAIR_SEPARATOR = re.compile(
    r",(?=\s*(?:" + "|".join(SCREENING_KINDS) + r")(?:"
    + re.escape(SECOND_SCREEN_MARK) + r")?(?::|$))"
)


def parse_screener_pair(text: str) -> tuple:
    """The two screeners `--pair A,B` names.

    Splits at the one comma followed by a kind (`llm`, `agent`, `human`,
    optionally `+second`) and then `:` or the end, so names may hold
    commas.

    Raises:
        ValueError: No such comma or more than one, a side
            `parse_screener_spec` refuses, or one screener twice.
    """
    commas = [m.start() for m in _PAIR_SEPARATOR.finditer(text)]
    if len(commas) != 1:
        raise ValueError(
            "give two screeners as A,B, each kind:name or a bare kind"
            + (" (more than one comma could separate them)"
               if len(commas) > 1 else "")
        )
    pair = (parse_screener_spec(text[: commas[0]]),
            parse_screener_spec(text[commas[0] + 1:]))
    if pair[0] == pair[1]:
        raise ValueError("give two different screeners")
    return pair
@

\paragraph{Which verdict counts.}
A screener can screen a paper more than once, and only the latest
verdict counts: a screener who changed their mind meant the second
one.  Latest is by the time of the verdict ([[at]]), else by the time
Scholar stored it, and on equal times the record stored later wins
(the list is kept in that order).  Only the screenings the review
module counts are read ([[counted_screenings]]): a reset sets aside
every screening made before it, since the decision it compared with is
gone, and malformed entries are skipped there.  The earlier verdicts
stay in the record; self-agreement is not computed.

The decision itself is not a verdict here.  Its first verdict is in the
list as a snapshot, taken when the first screening was recorded, and
that snapshot is the first screener's verdict; the decision may since
have been adjudicated, and comparing the second screener with an
outcome they helped bring about would make the two look more in
agreement than they were.  That is why the snapshot exists
(\cref{sec:screenings}).

<<agreement screeners>>=
def _verdict_time(record: dict) -> str:
    """When a verdict was made: its `at`, else when it was stored."""
    return str(record.get("at") or record.get("recorded_at") or "")


def screener_verdicts(decision: ReviewDecision) -> dict[Screener, dict]:
    """The latest counted verdict of each screener of `decision`."""
    latest: dict[Screener, dict] = {}
    for record in counted_screenings(decision):
        screener = screener_of(record)
        if screener is None:
            continue
        held = latest.get(screener)
        if held is None or not _iso_after(
            _verdict_time(held), _verdict_time(record)
        ):
            latest[screener] = record
    return latest


def unknown_origin_count(decisions: Iterable[ReviewDecision]) -> int:
    """How many counted verdicts of `decisions` have no known screener."""
    return sum(
        1
        for decision in decisions
        if decision.screenings
        for record in counted_screenings(decision)
        if screener_of(record) is None
    )
@

\paragraph{What a screener is called.}
The text names a screener by kind and name: \enquote{llm (m-1)},
\enquote{agent (agent-2)}, \enquote{unnamed agent}; a classifier's
second screen adds \enquote{second screen}, \enquote{llm (m-1),
second screen}, so that it reads apart from the model's first call.
One case needs more.  A snapshot of a decision that a human made,
confirmed or changed has kind [[human]] (\cref{sec:screenings}): the
status is the human's.
When the human confirmed a machine's call, though, the verdict is not
that of a human screening the paper alone, and a report that called it
\enquote{a human screener} would claim more independence than there
was.  Such a snapshot carries [[confirmed_from]], and a screener whose
records are all such snapshots is called \enquote{the human-confirmed
decision}, with the human's name and the machine that proposed it when
they are known and the same throughout.  A human screener with some
such records and some of their own says how many of its records are
the human-confirmed decision.

[[ScreenerProfile]] gathers what the text needs about each screener
over a set of records, once: how many records it screened, how many of
those are confirmed snapshots and by whom proposed, and the prompt
versions of its verdicts.

<<agreement screeners>>=
@dataclass
class ScreenerProfile:
    """What the text says about one screener over a set of records."""

    screener: Screener
    records: int = 0
    confirmed: int = 0
    proposers: set = field(default_factory=set)
    prompt_versions: set = field(default_factory=set)

    def add(self, record: dict) -> None:
        """Count one verdict of this screener."""
        self.records += 1
        if record.get("origin") == "decision" and record.get("confirmed_from"):
            self.confirmed += 1
            self.proposers.add(
                (record.get("confirmed_from"), record.get("confirmed_name"))
            )
        if record.get("prompt_version"):
            self.prompt_versions.add(record["prompt_version"])

    @property
    def label(self) -> str:
        """The screener's name in the text."""
        kind, name, second = self.screener
        base = f"{kind} ({name})" if name is not None else f"unnamed {kind}"
        if second:
            base += ", second screen"
        if not self.confirmed:
            return base
        if self.confirmed < self.records:
            return (
                f"{base} ({self.confirmed} of {self.records} records the "
                "human-confirmed decision)"
            )
        details = [f"by {name}"] if name is not None else []
        if len(self.proposers) == 1:
            (machine, machine_name), = self.proposers
            details.append(
                f"proposed by {machine}"
                + (f" {machine_name}" if machine_name else "")
            )
        return "the human-confirmed decision" + (
            f" ({'; '.join(details)})" if details else ""
        )


def screener_profiles(
    records: Iterable[dict[Screener, dict]],
) -> dict[Screener, ScreenerProfile]:
    """A profile of each screener in `records` (maps of `screener_verdicts`)."""
    profiles: dict[Screener, ScreenerProfile] = {}
    for verdicts in records:
        for screener, record in verdicts.items():
            profiles.setdefault(screener, ScreenerProfile(screener)).add(record)
    return profiles
@

Let's check the pairing rule on invented records: a screener's later
verdict replaces its earlier one, a reset sets aside what came before
it, a snapshot of unknown origin is no screener, and two unnamed agents
are one.

