\section{The agreement table}
\label{sec:agreement-table}

Two screeners who both screened the same $N$ records, each kept or
discarded, give a two-by-two table: $x_{11}$ records both kept,
$x_{12}$ kept by the first and discarded by the second, $x_{21}$ the
other way round, and $x_{22}$ discarded by both.  Everything this
section prints is computed from these four counts, and the counts are
printed too, so that a reader can compute any other measure from them.
What is printed, and what is not, follows what the sources read for the
design of the issue back; each is cited here with the part read.

\paragraph{Observed agreement.}
The share of records on which the two agree,
$P_o = (x_{11} + x_{22})/N$ (Shankar and Bangdiwala,
\enquote{Observer agreement paradoxes in 2x2 tables: comparison of
agreement measures}, \emph{BMC Medical Research Methodology} 14:100,
2014, \url{https://doi.org/10.1186/1471-2288-14-100}, Background).  It
is the plainest measure and the one a reader can check by hand, but it
says little on its own when one verdict is rare: \enquote{assume that
you have
10 relevant records per 1000 records.  You could then reach a raw
agreement of 99\% simply by excluding all records} (Vembye, Christensen,
M\o lgaard and Schytt, \enquote{Generative pretrained transformer models
can function as highly reliable second screeners of titles and abstracts
in systematic reviews: A proof of concept and common guidelines},
\emph{Psychological Methods}, 2025,
\url{https://doi.org/10.1037/met0000769}, p.~9 of the accepted
manuscript).

\paragraph{Positive and negative agreement.}
So the agreement on the two verdicts is also given apart, as the
proportions of specific agreement: positive agreement
$P_{\mathrm{pos}} = a / [(A^+ + B^+)/2]$ and negative agreement
$P_{\mathrm{neg}} = d / [(A^- + B^-)/2]$, where $a$ counts the records
both rated positive, $d$ those both rated negative, and $A^+$, $B^+$
each rater's positive count (Tan, Yeh, Adusumilli and Travis,
\enquote{Quantifying Interrater Agreement and Reliability Between
Thoracic Pathologists: Paradoxical Behavior of Cohen's Kappa in the
Presence of a High Prevalence of the Histopathologic Feature in Lung
Cancer}, \emph{JTO Clinical and Research Reports} 5(1):100618, 2024,
\url{https://doi.org/10.1016/j.jtocrr.2023.100618}, Methods).  With
\enquote{kept} as positive, $a = x_{11}$, $d = x_{22}$,
$A^+ + B^+ = 2x_{11} + x_{12} + x_{21}$ and $A^- + B^- = 2x_{22} +
x_{12} + x_{21}$, so
\[
  P_{\mathrm{pos}} = \frac{2x_{11}}{2x_{11} + x_{12} + x_{21}},
  \qquad
  P_{\mathrm{neg}} = \frac{2x_{22}}{2x_{22} + x_{12} + x_{21}}.
\]
Positive agreement leaves out the records both screeners discarded, so
it answers how far the two agree on what to keep.  The pair goes back
to Cicchetti and Feinstein, who suggested two separate indexes
\enquote{to quantify agreement in the positive and negative
decisions}; their paper was not
read, so this is a citation of a citation, through Shankar and
Bangdiwala (Background).  A proportion with nothing to count, when
neither screener kept a record, is undefined, not zero.

\paragraph{Cohen's kappa.}
Kappa compares the observed agreement with the agreement chance would
give: $\kappa = (P_o - P_e)/(1 - P_e)$, with $P_e$ the chance-expected
agreement (Shankar and Bangdiwala, Background).  For two categories,
Vach and Gerke write $P_e$ with the two screeners' shares of positive
verdicts $\pi_A$ and $\pi_B$ as $e = \pi_A\pi_B + (1 - \pi_A)(1 -
\pi_B)$ (Vach and Gerke, \enquote{Gwet's AC1 is not a substitute for
Cohen's kappa -- A comparison of basic properties}, \emph{MethodsX}
10:102212, 2023, \url{https://doi.org/10.1016/j.mex.2023.102212},
Methods), here $\pi_A = (x_{11} + x_{12})/N$ and $\pi_B = (x_{11} +
x_{21})/N$.

Kappa's value depends on how common each verdict is: its
\enquote{behavior is subject to changes in prevalence}, and with
imbalanced marginals it can be low although the observed agreement is
high (Shankar and Bangdiwala, Background and Results; their scenario~3,
$x = 90, 5, 5, 0$, has $P_o = 0.90$ and $\kappa = -0.05$).  Whether
that is a fault is disputed.  Shankar and Bangdiwala recommend another
measure, their B-statistic (Discussion and Conclusions), and
Wongpakaran and co-authors prefer Gwet's AC1 (cited below); Vach and
Gerke answer that for a fixed agreement rate kappa falls as the
prevalence moves from 0.5, \enquote{which is in accordance with the aim
to take into account that high agreement rates get more likely}
(Discussion), and that Gwet's AC1, proposed as an alternative to kappa
and \enquote{becoming increasingly popular}, \enquote{should not be
seen as a substitute for Cohen's kappa} (Abstract).  Scholar prints kappa,
whose dependence on prevalence Vach and Gerke defend as intended, but
never alone: with the
counts, the specific agreement above, and two indices that show the
imbalance.  The \emph{prevalence index} $\mathrm{PI} = (x_{11} -
x_{22})/N$ and the \emph{bias index} $\mathrm{BI} = (x_{12} -
x_{21})/N$ were proposed by Byrt and co-authors to be reported with
kappa (Shankar and Bangdiwala, Background and Methods; Byrt's paper
was not read, so this too is a citation of a citation).  AC1, the
prevalence- and bias-adjusted kappa (which is $2P_o - 1$, a rescaled
observed agreement) and the other alternatives are not computed.

Kappa has no value when $P_e = 1$: then both screeners gave a single
verdict throughout, the same one, and the formula divides by zero.
When only one of them did, the formula gives exactly 0 whatever the
agreement: with $\pi_A = 1$, say, $P_o = \pi_B = P_e$.  A 0 there
would read as agreement no better than chance, while it only says that
one screener never used one of the verdicts.  Where a marginal count
was zero, in their data with two raters in full agreement, Wongpakaran
and co-authors report that kappa \enquote{gave a `0' value} and that
SPSS could not compute it at all (Wongpakaran, Wongpakaran, Wedding
and Gwet, \enquote{A comparison of Cohen's Kappa and Gwet's AC1 when
calculating inter-rater reliability coefficients: a study conducted
with personality disorder
samples}, \emph{BMC Medical Research Methodology} 13:61, 2013,
\url{https://doi.org/10.1186/1471-2288-13-61}, Results).  So Scholar
calls kappa undefined whenever a screener gave only one verdict, and
says which case it is; it never prints 0 for it.

\paragraph{What is not printed.}
Kappa gets no verbal label (\enquote{moderate}, \enquote{good}): the
published scales disagree, Landis and Koch, Altman and Fleiss drawing
different cut points and words (Wongpakaran and co-authors, Table~7:
0.41--0.60 is \enquote{Moderate} for Landis and Koch and for Altman,
while Fleiss's 0.40--0.75 is \enquote{Intermediate to Good}), and no
source read validates any of them.  Kappa gets no interval either: the
one source read that gives an analytical interval, for the two-by-two
case only (Reichenheim, \enquote{Confidence intervals for the kappa
statistic}, \emph{The Stata Journal} 4(4):421--428, 2004, p.~421),
was found in the design of the issue not to reproduce its own worked
example (p.~424) with its equation~(1) as printed, so it is left out
until the original it names, by Fleiss, Cohen and Everitt, is read.  No
interval is given for the specific agreement, for which no source was
read.  And for more than two categories only the table and the observed
agreement are given: the sources read state kappa for two categories
only.

[[AgreementTable]] holds a pair's counts for one question, in a square
table: row $i$, column $j$ of [[cells]] counts the records the first
screener put in the $i$th category and the second in the $j$th, with
the categories in
[[labels]] order, the positive one first.  Everything else is computed
from the cells when asked, so a table is never out of step with its
counts.  [[without_category]] counts the records both screened that
this question could not use (below), and [[interval]] holds the
interval of the observed agreement, which only a sample gets
(\cref{sec:agreement-samples}).

<<agreement table>>=
@dataclass(frozen=True)
class AgreementTable:
    """Two screeners' verdicts of the same records, counted.

    `cells[i][j]` counts the records screener `a` put in `labels[i]`
    and screener `b` in `labels[j]`; for two labels the first is the
    positive verdict (kept, bearing). `without_category` counts the
    records both screened that the question could not use; `interval`
    is the 95% interval of the observed agreement, for a sample only.
    """

    a: Screener
    b: Screener
    question: str
    labels: tuple
    cells: tuple
    without_category: int = 0
    interval: tuple | None = None

    <<agreement table measures>>
@

The observed agreement needs only the diagonal.

<<agreement table measures>>=
@property
def n(self) -> int:
    """How many records both screeners judged on this question."""
    return sum(sum(row) for row in self.cells)

@property
def agreeing(self) -> int:
    """How many of them the two put in the same category."""
    return sum(self.cells[i][i] for i in range(len(self.labels)))

@property
def observed(self) -> float | None:
    """The observed agreement, or None without records."""
    return self.agreeing / self.n if self.n else None

@property
def is_binary(self) -> bool:
    """Whether the table is two by two (the only case with kappa)."""
    return len(self.labels) == 2
@

The specific agreement follows the formulas above.  The indices are
signed, as defined: a negative prevalence index says that the negative
verdict was the more common one.

<<agreement table measures>>=
def _specific(self, both: int) -> float | None:
    if not self.is_binary:
        return None
    off_diagonal = self.cells[0][1] + self.cells[1][0]
    denominator = 2 * both + off_diagonal
    return 2 * both / denominator if denominator else None

@property
def positive_agreement(self) -> float | None:
    """Positive agreement; None if undefined or not two by two."""
    return self._specific(self.cells[0][0]) if self.is_binary else None

@property
def negative_agreement(self) -> float | None:
    """Negative agreement; None if undefined or not two by two."""
    return self._specific(self.cells[1][1]) if self.is_binary else None

@property
def prevalence_index(self) -> float | None:
    """(x11 - x22) / N; None without records or not two by two."""
    if not self.is_binary or not self.n:
        return None
    return (self.cells[0][0] - self.cells[1][1]) / self.n

@property
def bias_index(self) -> float | None:
    """(x12 - x21) / N; None without records or not two by two."""
    if not self.is_binary or not self.n:
        return None
    return (self.cells[0][1] - self.cells[1][0]) / self.n
@

Kappa is computed only when both screeners used both verdicts; the
reason it is not is a sentence the text prints as it is.

<<agreement table measures>>=
@property
def kappa_undefined_reason(self) -> str | None:
    """Why kappa has no value, or None when it has one."""
    if not self.is_binary:
        return "not computed for more than two categories"
    if not self.n:
        return "no records screened by both"
    (x11, x12), (x21, x22) = self.cells
    one_verdict = [
        positives in (0, self.n) for positives in (x11 + x12, x11 + x21)
    ]
    if all(one_verdict):
        return "both screeners gave only one verdict"
    if any(one_verdict):
        return "one screener gave only one verdict"
    return None

@property
def chance_agreement(self) -> float | None:
    """The chance-expected agreement P_e, two by two only."""
    if not self.is_binary or not self.n:
        return None
    (x11, x12), (x21, _) = self.cells
    pi_a, pi_b = (x11 + x12) / self.n, (x11 + x21) / self.n
    return pi_a * pi_b + (1 - pi_a) * (1 - pi_b)

@property
def kappa(self) -> float | None:
    """Cohen's kappa, or None when `kappa_undefined_reason` says why."""
    if self.kappa_undefined_reason is not None:
        return None
    expected = self.chance_agreement
    return (self.observed - expected) / (1 - expected)
@

The table is checked against the sources' own numbers.  Shankar and
Bangdiwala's scenarios 1 to 3 (their Table~2 for the cells and the
indices, Table~3 for $P_o$, $P_e$ and kappa, all to two decimals, three
for $P_e = 0.905$) cover a balanced table and the two imbalanced ones of
the first paradox; their bias index is $(x_{12} - x_{21})/N$ with the
cells in our order (scenario~1: $0.03$).  Tan and co-authors' lepidic
and acinar patterns (Results: 50 sections, $P_{\mathrm{pos}} = 18 /
22.5$ and $P_{\mathrm{neg}} = 23 / 27.5$; $41/44$ and $3/6$) check the
specific agreement; the split of their disagreements between the two
off-diagonal cells is not given and does not matter.  Then the undefined
cases.

