\section{Three questions over a set of records}
\label{sec:agreement-questions}

Two screeners can agree on more than kept or discarded, and a review
that classifies its records asks two more questions of them.  Each
question reads one value from a verdict:
\begin{description}
\item[ [[status]] ] kept or discarded, the verdict itself.
\item[ [[bearing]] ] whether the record bears on the claim: its
  category ([[audit_category]] of the tags, the rule of the audit table)
  is one of those the audit table counts as bearing, [[supports-claim]]
  and [[qualifies-claim]] ([[AUDIT_ORDER_BEARING]]), or one of the
  others.  It is a two-category question, so kappa applies.
\item[ [[category]] ] the category itself, one of the four in
  [[AUDIT_CATEGORIES]]: a four-by-four table and its observed agreement.
\end{description}
A verdict without a category has no value for the last two questions:
a human screening may carry none (\cref{sec:sessions-screen} in the CLI
module), and old decisions have none.  A record whose verdict from
either screener lacks a value drops out of that question and is
counted as [[without_category]], so the text can say how many records
the numbers leave out.

<<agreement questions>>=
QUESTIONS: dict[str, tuple] = {
    "status": ("kept", "discarded"),
    "bearing": ("bearing", "not bearing"),
    "category": tuple(AUDIT_CATEGORIES),
}


def verdict_value(record: dict, question: str) -> str | None:
    """What a verdict answers to `question` (`QUESTIONS`), or None."""
    if question == "status":
        return record.get("status")
    category = audit_category(list(record.get("tags") or []))
    if category is None or question == "category":
        return category
    if AUDIT_CATEGORIES[category] <= AUDIT_ORDER_BEARING:
        return "bearing"
    return "not bearing"
@

[[screening_agreement]] counts one pair's table over a set of records.
A record is one decision: copies of one work kept apart under one id
are counted as records each, as the per-decision CSV counts them, and
the records of pooled sessions are counted side by side.  The records
come as the maps [[screener_verdicts]] gives, computed once by the
caller, since a report asks several questions of several pairs over the
same records.

<<agreement questions>>=
def screening_agreement(
    records: Iterable[dict[Screener, dict]],
    a: Screener,
    b: Screener,
    question: str = "status",
) -> AgreementTable:
    """The table of screeners `a` and `b` on `question` over `records`.

    `records` holds one `screener_verdicts` map per record; records
    without a verdict of both screeners are left out, and those where
    either verdict has no value for the question are counted in
    `without_category`.
    """
    labels = QUESTIONS[question]
    index = {label: i for i, label in enumerate(labels)}
    cells = [[0] * len(labels) for _ in labels]
    without = 0
    for verdicts in records:
        if a not in verdicts or b not in verdicts:
            continue
        row = index.get(verdict_value(verdicts[a], question))
        column = index.get(verdict_value(verdicts[b], question))
        if row is None or column is None:
            without += 1
            continue
        cells[row][column] += 1
    return AgreementTable(
        a, b, question, labels, tuple(tuple(r) for r in cells), without
    )
@

The pairs a set of records holds are every two screeners with at least
one record in common, in a fixed order (by kind, as listed in
[[SCREENING_KINDS]], then by name, the unnamed first), so that the same
session always prints its pairs alike.  The snapshot's screener counts
like any other: the decision's first verdict is one side of most pairs.

<<agreement questions>>=
def _screener_order(screener: Screener) -> tuple:
    kind, name, second = screener
    return (SCREENING_KINDS.index(kind), name is not None, name or "", second)


def screener_pairs(
    records: Iterable[dict[Screener, dict]],
) -> list[tuple[Screener, Screener]]:
    """Every pair of screeners with a record in common, in a fixed order."""
    pairs: set = set()
    for verdicts in records:
        ordered = sorted(verdicts, key=_screener_order)
        pairs.update(combinations(ordered, 2))
    return sorted(
        pairs, key=lambda p: (_screener_order(p[0]), _screener_order(p[1]))
    )
@

Let's check the questions on three invented records screened by a
classifier and an agent, plus one only the agent screened: the agent's
verdict without a category drops out of the category questions and is
